i don't think i've ever  had a block like that it's pretty good  defense  [Music]  this is evan mobley with the cleveland  cavaliers i'm going to show you the ins  and out of how i play defense this is  the breakdown  yeah we're in our zone actually  on this play and so trey's at the top  i guess he sees me so he wanted to go  one-on-one  my job was just to really contain him  and then you see he's driving to the  paint so i just try to use my length and  contest without fouling as you see i  have a high hand early so he doesn't  think to shoot the ball and then as he  starts dribbling i try to keep a  good enough distance to where i can  like if he steps back like he did a  little bit i could come up and still uh  contest a shot and then if he drives i  can also  be there and stay in front and and get  the block at the end i know that uh j.a  and lowry are behind me two seven  footers so  if he's going to the basket i know  me  lowry or jay he's going to have a pretty  good contest on it he ended up putting  the ball on my side and i got the block  how we play our defense allows me to be  in a lot of different spots in different  areas of the floor and  almost always be there for the contest  it's crazy that i defend 17 shots a game  and  already defended 30 shots twice this  year i don't really know  these metrics and  to hear these metrics from you guys is  just crazy the key to holding my  match-ups to under 46  within 10 feet is  really just staying vertical and  holding holding your spot in the paint  and contesting at the top  and always contest without fouling  never leave your feet first and jump  second this is crazy that  as a rookie i'm i'm out here  doing that right now this game was a  tough game close game all the way down  to the wire tatum got the ball  and he started pushing it i was like i  knew he was going to go like the whole  way probably and try to get the layup so  i was just really tracking behind him  the whole time  and then my teammates did a good job of  slowing him down a little bit and then i  just went and jumped for the blocking  and got it i thought he was gonna shoot  it off the backboard i was going to  go for the pin but he ended up shooting  it in the middle of the hoop and  i got there quick enough to not get  goaltending i don't think i've ever  had a block like that  like where it was right over the rim  it's pretty good defense  me coming up with this big block uh  definitely shifted a lot of the momentum  to ours our side and i feel like this  kind of  was like a turning point in the fourth  quarter to like get us to win me being  a  top 25 and  forcing turnovers is  uh  another crazy metric that i didn't  realize i feel like my length and  how well i move my feet really caused  most of the turnovers this metric  uh  definitely shows the value of  my defensive presence because  a lot of times i force turnovers but i  don't necessarily get this deal i feel  like i do that a lot  as well as like altered shots that are  not necessarily blocks but  me just being there changing the shot up  really affect the game in  ways that like a normal statue wouldn't  show you a hero i knew  he was coming off like a good couple of  games i think lowry happened to be  guarding him and then the big set of  screens so i switched and then  i knew he was going to come at me so i  just stayed down and tried to stay ready  basically crab walking keeping one foot  down ready to move either way  and  after he stepped back i i seen him  pulling up so  just  jumped his eyes and as hard as i can and  got the tip usually  uh i stick my arm out and basically uh  use that as like a measuring stick to  really judge how far i am and  if i'm if i can almost touch them i feel  like if they pull up right now i could  probably get a good contest  and then also if they wanted to drive  i can  react in time with the space i have  to stay in front of my man and like  contain them thanks for watching film me  on the breakdown shout out bleacher  report and nba court optics

